Say a new release exists in the app menu, not only on the About page.

The daily release check has been writing aerium.update.latest_version and
aerium.update.latest_url since it landed, and the only thing that ever read
them was a row on chrome://settings/help. That row is correct and nobody sees
it: the About page is where you go once, to find out what version you are on,
and there is no reason to return.

Android does not have this problem - it posts a notification - so the two
platforms have been finding out about the same release in very different ways.

So the app menu gets a row, in the place Chrome puts its own update row, with
the same icon and the same emphasis. The difference is what it does. Chrome's
IDC_UPGRADE_DIALOG relaunches to apply an update that has already been
downloaded and installed by an updater Aerium does not ship; this opens the
release page, because downloading it is the user's job and always was. Reusing
IDC_UPGRADE_DIALOG would have been shorter and would have meant the menu item
lied about what clicking it does.

Not wired through BrowserCommandController
------------------------------------------
AppMenuModel::ExecuteCommand and ::IsCommandIdEnabled both delegate to
chrome::ExecuteCommandWithContext and chrome::IsCommandEnabled, which answer
for ids the command controller knows. Registering another one means touching
browser_command_controller.cc, browser_commands.cc and their headers to add a
command that exists in exactly one menu and has no accelerator, no policy and
no enabled/disabled states worth tracking.

Both functions already have a place to intercept before that delegation -
ExecuteCommand does it for IDC_VIEW_PASSWORDS, IsCommandIdEnabled for
IDC_NEW_INCOGNITO_WINDOW - so this uses them. The row is only built when there
is something to open, so "enabled" is unconditionally true, and the execute
branch returns before LogMenuMetrics because there is no WrenchMenu histogram
bucket for it and inventing one would be worse than recording nothing.

The prefs are read as string literals, the same way prefs_util.cc reads them:
including aerium_update_checker.h pulls the network service into whatever
includes it, and app_menu_model.cc is not a place to do that. Both prefs are
written together and both are cleared when the check finds this build is the
newest, so testing either for empty is enough - and an offer that has been
superseded disappears on its own.

The URL is checked for https at the point it becomes a navigation. The checker
only writes it after matching the release API's response shape, but that
validation happened in a different process lifetime, and this is where a string
from the network turns into a page load.

